.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_IO 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_get_fs_writer ,
.Nm xft_get_raw_writer ,
.Nm xft_writer_write ,
.Nm xft_writer_flush ,
.Nm xft_get_fs_reader ,
.Nm xft_get_raw_reader ,
.Nm xft_read_from_reader ,
.Nm xft_get_stdin ,
.Nm xft_get_stdout ,
.Nm xft_get_stderr ,
.Nm xft_map_file ,
.Nm xft_unmap_file
.Nd buffered readers and writers, standard handles and file mapping
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In io.h
.Fd #define BUFSIZE 4096
.Ft t_writer
.Fn xft_get_fs_writer "t_buffer buffer" "t_i32 fd"
.Ft t_writer
.Fn xft_get_raw_writer "t_buffer buffer" "t_u8 *external" "t_size external_valid"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_writer_write "t_writer *writer" "t_buffer buf"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_writer_flush "t_writer *writer"
.Ft t_reader
.Fn xft_get_fs_reader "t_buffer buffer" "t_i32 fd"
.Ft t_reader
.Fn xft_get_raw_reader "t_buffer buffer" "t_buffer external"
.Ft t_result
.Fn xft_read_from_reader "t_reader *reader" "t_u8 *dst" "t_size len" "t_size *total"
.Ft t_i32
.Fn xft_get_stdin void
.Ft t_i32
.Fn xft_get_stdout void
.Ft t_i32
.Fn xft_get_stderr void
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_map_file "const t_u8 *name" "t_u32 flags"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_unmap_file "t_buffer file"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Readers and writers stage data in a caller-supplied
.Vt t_buffer .
They never allocate and have no destructor;
the staging buffer must outlive the handle.
Each handle carries a pointer to a static vtable selecting its backend:
a file descriptor
.Pq Dq fs
or a caller-owned memory region
.Pq Dq raw .
.Pp
.Dv BUFSIZE
is a suggested staging buffer size.
It is defined only if not already defined.
.Ss Types
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Vt t_iovec
.Fa mem
and
.Fa size ;
the element type passed to
.Fn xft_writev .
.It Vt t_writer
.Fa vtable ,
the staging
.Fa buffer ,
.Fa end
(bytes currently staged) and the backend union
.Fa as .
.Fa as Ns .fs_writer
holds
.Fa fd .
.Fa as Ns .mem_writer
holds
.Fa external ,
its capacity
.Fa external_valid ,
and
.Fa external_size ,
the number of bytes stored in it so far.
.It Vt t_reader
.Fa vtable ,
the staging
.Fa buffer ,
.Fa end
(read offset into the staged data),
.Fa valid
(bytes staged) and the backend union
.Fa as .
.Fa as Ns .fs_reader
holds
.Fa fd .
.Fa as Ns .mem_reader
holds the source buffer
.Fa external
and
.Fa drained ,
the number of source bytes consumed.
.It Vt t_writer_vtable
Function pointers
.Fa flush
and
.Fa drain .
.It Vt t_reader_vtable
Function pointers
.Fa unbufered_fill
(sic) and
.Fa fill .
.El
.Ss Writers
.Fn xft_get_fs_writer
returns a writer that stages into
.Fa buffer
and drains to file descriptor
.Fa fd .
.Pp
.Fn xft_get_raw_writer
returns a writer that stages into
.Fa buffer
and drains into
.Fa external ,
which has room for
.Fa external_valid
bytes.
The number of bytes delivered so far is
.Fa writer Ns .as.mem_writer.external_size .
.Pp
.Fn xft_writer_write
appends
.Fa buf
to the writer.
If it fits in the free space of the staging buffer it is only copied there.
Otherwise the staged bytes and a leading part of
.Fa buf
are drained in one vectored operation, and the remaining tail of
.Fa buf ,
.Pq end + buf.size
modulo
.Fa buffer Ns .size
bytes, is copied to the start of the staging buffer.
The fs backend calls
.Fn xft_writev
until everything is written.
The raw backend first checks that
.Fa external
has room for the whole drain.
.Pp
.Fn xft_writer_flush
writes out the staged bytes and empties the staging buffer.
The fs backend calls
.Fn xft_write
until all staged bytes are written.
The raw backend fails without copying anything if
.Fa external
lacks room for them.
Nothing is flushed implicitly; staged data is lost unless
.Fn xft_writer_flush
is called before the program exits.
.Ss Readers
.Fn xft_get_fs_reader
returns a reader that fills
.Fa buffer
from file descriptor
.Fa fd .
.Fn xft_get_raw_reader
returns a reader that fills
.Fa buffer
from the memory in
.Fa external .
.Pp
.Fn xft_read_from_reader
copies up to
.Fa len
bytes into
.Fa dst
and stores the count in
.Pf * Fa total .
It keeps reading until
.Fa len
bytes have been copied or the source reaches end of file,
so a short count means end of file;
.Pf * Fa total
is 0 once the source is exhausted.
If
.Fa len
is at least
.Fa buffer Ns .size ,
the staged bytes are copied out and the rest is read directly into
.Fa dst ,
bypassing the staging buffer.
Otherwise the staging buffer is refilled with one
.Fn xft_read
call
(or one copy from
.Fa external )
whenever it runs empty.
.Ss Standard handles
.Fn xft_get_stdin ,
.Fn xft_get_stdout
and
.Fn xft_get_stderr
return the handle for the standard streams, suitable as the
.Fa fd
argument above.
On Linux these are 0, 1 and 2.
On Windows they are the results of
.Fn GetStdHandle
truncated to
.Vt t_i32 .
.Ss File mapping
.Fn xft_map_file
maps the file
.Fa name
read-only and private.
.Fa flags
is passed to
.Fn xft_open
and should be
.Dv O_RDONLY .
The descriptor is closed before returning; the mapping stays valid.
.Pp
.Fn xft_unmap_file
unmaps a buffer returned by
.Fn xft_map_file .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_writer_write ,
.Fn xft_writer_flush
and
.Fn xft_read_from_reader
return
.Dv OK
on success and
.Dv KO
on failure.
For fs handles, failure means a
.Fn xft_read
returned a negative value, or a
.Fn xft_write
or
.Fn xft_writev
returned zero or a negative value.
For raw writers, failure means
.Fa external
is too small.
Raw readers never fail.
.Pp
The constructors return the handle by value and cannot fail.
.Pp
.Fn xft_map_file
returns the mapped bytes and the file size, or a zeroed
.Vt t_buffer
if
.Fn xft_stat ,
.Fn xft_open
or the mapping fails.
An empty file cannot be mapped and also yields a zeroed buffer.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy standard input to standard output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	xft_main(const t_any *__restrict__ const sp)
{
	t_u8		inbuf[BUFSIZE];
	t_u8		outbuf[BUFSIZE];
	t_u8		chunk[512];
	t_reader	r;
	t_writer	w;
	t_size		n;

	(void)sp;
	r = xft_get_fs_reader(xft_fatptr(inbuf, BUFSIZE), xft_get_stdin());
	w = xft_get_fs_writer(xft_fatptr(outbuf, BUFSIZE), xft_get_stdout());
	while (xft_read_from_reader(&r, chunk, sizeof(chunk), &n) && n != 0)
		if (!xft_writer_write(&w, xft_fatptr(chunk, n)))
			xft_exit(1);
	xft_exit(!xft_writer_flush(&w));
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
